In the context of quantum-geometric constructions, a local band gap plays a crucial role in determining the stability and topological properties of the system. When a band gap is present, it can amplify the response of the system to external perturbations, leading to enhanced observable effects. On the other hand, a vanishing band gap can serve as a validity condition for the extraction of quantum-geometric quantities, ensuring that the system remains in a stable and topologically non-trivial state. The opposite consequences arise because a vanishing band gap can destabilize the system, making it difficult to measure quantum-geometric quantities accurately, while a non-vanishing band gap can amplify these effects, making them easier to observe.

