Explanation:
The Gibbs-measure-over-histories framework, as discussed in the second paper (Doc 2), involves the use of histograms to approximate the target distribution. In this framework, the support-point count M in the discretization approximation is a scalar control parameter whose refinement (or vanishing) is meant to recover an idealized exact object. However, unlike the discretization approximation and unadjusted HMC, the limits in the Gibbs-measure-over-histories framework can fail to be unique due to the inherent nature of the histogram-based approach.

