{"answer_pre":"The perfect one-cold Poisson channel (OCPC) avoids the O(sqrt(T)) second-order penalty that the entropy-constrained Gaussian channel's analogue would exhibit if measured on a comparable dispersion scale because the OCPC's achievable performance converges to a limiting information quantity in a manner that is independent of the duration T, whereas the entropy-constrained Gaussian channel's achievable performance converges to a limiting information quantity in a manner that is dependent on the duration T. This difference is due to the fact that the OCPC is a continuous-time channel with zero dispersion, which means that the channel's performance is not affected by the duration of the input signal. On the other hand, the entropy-constrained Gaussian channel is a discrete-time channel with finite dispersion, which means that the channel's performance is affected by the duration of the input signal. As a result, the OCPC's achievable performance converges to a limiting information quantity in a manner that is independent of the duration T, whereas the entropy-constrained Gaussian channel's achievable performance converges to a limiting information quantity in a manner that is dependent on the duration T."}

